Accept (no-op) mode changes on the 9p mount instead of failing.

TinyEMU's 9p server `fs_setattr` returned `P9_ENOTSUP` for `P9_SETATTR_MODE`,
and `chmod(2)` is a wasi-libc stub anyway (WASI preview1 has no chmod). Guests
therefore saw `chmod` fail with "Protocol error" (EPROTO) on `/workspace`, which
breaks ordinary tooling — npm's `node_modules/.bin/*` shims, `git`, `install`,
etc.

Making the mode branch succeed is safe on this mount: the Linux 9p client
updates the guest inode's in-memory mode from the request, and that is what the
guest's permission/exec checks consult (the host mode bits behind the WASI
preopen are not meaningful — creation mode bits are already dropped). The
`ctime_updated` flag also short-circuits the `P9_SETATTR_CTIME` branch, which
had the same `P9_ENOTSUP` stub.

The `P9_SETATTR_UID|GID` (chown) branch is left as-is; extend the same way only
if a workload needs `chown`.

diff --git a/fs_disk.c b/fs_disk.c
--- a/fs_disk.c
+++ b/fs_disk.c
@@ -582,10 +582,14 @@
     }
     /* must be done after uid change for suid */
     if (mask & P9_SETATTR_MODE) {
-        return P9_ENOTSUP; // TODO
-//        if (chmod(f->path, mode) < 0)
-//            return -errno_to_p9(errno);
-//        ctime_updated = TRUE;
+        /* WASI preview1 has no chmod (wasi-libc stubs it with ENOTSUP), so a
+           real chmod() cannot be issued here. Accept the request instead of
+           failing with P9_ENOTSUP: the Linux 9p client updates the guest
+           inode's in-memory mode from the request, which is what permission
+           and exec checks use. Tools that chmod on the mount (npm's
+           node_modules/.bin/*, git, install, ...) therefore work instead of
+           failing with "Protocol error". */
+        ctime_updated = TRUE;
     }
     if (mask & P9_SETATTR_SIZE) {
         if (truncate(f->path, size) < 0)
